Wireshark Interface

The Wireshark interface is generally divided into three important sections.

3.1 Packet List Pane

The Packet List Pane displays captured packets in rows.

Common columns include:

Column	Meaning
No.	Packet/frame number
Time	Time at which packet was captured
Source	Source address
Destination	Destination address
Protocol	Detected protocol
Length	Packet/frame length
Info	Short description of the packet

Example:

No.   Time       Source          Destination       Protocol   Length   Info
1     0.000000   192.168.1.10    192.168.1.1       ARP        42       Who has...
2     0.001234   192.168.1.1     192.168.1.10      ARP        42       192.168.1.1 is at...
3     0.002345   192.168.1.10    8.8.8.8           ICMP       98       Echo request
3.2 Packet Details Pane

This section displays the selected packet as a protocol tree.

For example:

Frame
Ethernet II
Internet Protocol Version 4
Transmission Control Protocol
Hypertext Transfer Protocol

Each section can be expanded to view individual fields.

3.3 Packet Bytes Pane

This pane displays the raw bytes of the selected packet.

It normally contains:

Hexadecimal representation
ASCII representation

This is useful when examining the exact bytes transmitted over the network.

4. How a Packet is Structured

A typical packet can contain multiple protocol layers.

A simplified structure is:

Ethernet
    ↓
IP
    ↓
TCP / UDP / ICMP
    ↓
Application Protocol

For example:

Ethernet II
    ↓
IPv4
    ↓
TCP
    ↓
HTTP

Another example:

Ethernet II
    ↓
IPv4
    ↓
UDP
    ↓
DNS

Not every packet contains every layer.

For example, ARP operates directly at the link layer and is not carried inside TCP or UDP.

5. OSI Model and Wireshark

The OSI model contains seven layers.

OSI Layer	Name	Example Protocols
7	Application	HTTP, DNS, DHCP, FTP
6	Presentation	Encryption/encoding concepts
5	Session	Session management
4	Transport	TCP, UDP
3	Network	IPv4, IPv6, ICMP
2	Data Link	Ethernet, ARP
1	Physical	Cables, radio, signals

Wireshark mainly exposes information from Layers 2–7.

A common packet hierarchy is:

Layer 2 → Ethernet
Layer 3 → IPv4
Layer 4 → TCP
Layer 7 → HTTP
6. Ethernet II

Ethernet is commonly used at the Data Link Layer.

An Ethernet II frame contains fields such as:

Field	Description
Destination MAC	MAC address of receiving device on the local link
Source MAC	MAC address of transmitting device
EtherType	Identifies the encapsulated protocol
Payload	Data carried by Ethernet
FCS	Frame integrity field, when available in the capture

Common EtherTypes include:

0x0800 → IPv4
0x86DD → IPv6
0x0806 → ARP
MAC Address

A MAC address identifies a network interface at the local link layer.

Example:

00:11:22:33:44:55

Important:

MAC addresses are primarily used for local network delivery, while IP addresses provide logical addressing across networks.

7. ARP
Address Resolution Protocol

ARP is used in IPv4 networks to discover the MAC address associated with an IPv4 address on the local network.

Suppose:

Computer IP: 192.168.1.10
Gateway IP:  192.168.1.1

The computer may first need to discover the gateway's MAC address.

It sends an ARP Request:

Who has 192.168.1.1?
Tell 192.168.1.10

The gateway responds:

192.168.1.1 is at AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
Important ARP Fields
Field	Meaning
Hardware Type	Type of link-layer hardware
Protocol Type	Network protocol being resolved
Opcode	Request or reply
Sender MAC	MAC address of sender
Sender IP	IP address of sender
Target MAC	Target MAC address
Target IP	IP address being resolved
Wireshark Filter
arp

This displays ARP packets.

8. IPv4

IPv4 is a Network Layer protocol.

Example IPv4 address:

192.168.1.10

Important IPv4 header fields include:

Field	Description
Version	IPv4 uses version 4
IHL	IPv4 header length
DSCP/ECN	Traffic and congestion-related information
Total Length	Complete IP packet length
Identification	Used for fragmentation
Flags	Fragmentation control
Fragment Offset	Position of a fragment
TTL	Time To Live
Protocol	Identifies next protocol
Header Checksum	IPv4 header integrity check
Source Address	Sender IP
Destination Address	Receiver IP
Protocol Field

The IPv4 Protocol field identifies the next protocol.

Common values:

1  → ICMP
6  → TCP
17 → UDP
9. IPv6

IPv6 is the successor to IPv4.

IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long.

Example:

2001:db8::1

Important IPv6 fields include:

Field	Description
Version	Always 6
Traffic Class	Traffic priority/congestion information
Flow Label	Identifies packet flows
Payload Length	Length after IPv6 header
Next Header	Identifies next protocol/header
Hop Limit	Similar purpose to IPv4 TTL
Source Address	Sender IPv6 address
Destination Address	Receiver IPv6 address

IPv6 does not use ARP. Instead, IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP).

10. ICMP

ICMP stands for:

Internet Control Message Protocol

ICMP is mainly used for network diagnostics and control messages.

The ping command commonly uses ICMP.

Example:

ping 8.8.8.8

The client sends:

ICMP Echo Request

The destination responds with:

ICMP Echo Reply
Useful Filters
icmp

Display all ICMP packets.

icmp.type == 8

Display IPv4 Echo Requests.

icmp.type == 0

Display IPv4 Echo Replies.

Important ICMP Fields
Field	Description
Type	Identifies ICMP message type
Code	Provides additional information
Checksum	Error detection
Identifier	Helps identify an echo exchange
Sequence Number	Identifies individual echo messages
Data	Payload
11. TCP

TCP stands for:

Transmission Control Protocol

TCP is a connection-oriented transport protocol.

TCP provides:

Reliable delivery
Ordered delivery
Error detection
Flow control
Retransmission
Connection management

TCP uses ports to identify applications/services.

Examples:

HTTP  → 80
HTTPS → 443
SSH   → 22
DNS   → commonly 53
TCP Header Fields
Field	Description
Source Port	Port used by sender
Destination Port	Port used by receiver
Sequence Number	Tracks byte position
Acknowledgment Number	Indicates next expected byte when ACK is valid
Header Length	TCP header size
Flags	Connection/control information
Window Size	Receiver's advertised receive capacity
Checksum	Error detection
Urgent Pointer	Used with URG
Options	MSS, SACK, timestamps, window scaling, etc.
Payload	Application data
12. TCP Three-Way Handshake

Before sending normal application data, TCP usually establishes a connection using a three-way handshake.

Step 1 – SYN

Client sends:

SYN

Purpose:

Request connection
Synchronize sequence numbers
Step 2 – SYN + ACK

Server responds:

SYN + ACK

Purpose:

Acknowledge client's SYN
Send server's own SYN
Step 3 – ACK

Client sends:

ACK

The TCP connection is now established.

Simplified:

Client                    Server
  |                         |
  | -------- SYN ---------> |
  |                         |
  | <----- SYN + ACK ------ |
  |                         |
  | -------- ACK ---------> |
  |                         |
  |      Connection         |
  |      Established         |
Wireshark Filter
tcp.flags.syn == 1

More specifically:

tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0

This is useful for finding initial SYN packets.

13. TCP Flags

TCP contains several important flags.

Flag	Meaning
SYN	Starts/synchronizes a TCP connection
ACK	Acknowledges received information
FIN	Gracefully closes a connection
RST	Resets a connection
PSH	Requests prompt delivery of buffered data
URG	Indicates urgent pointer significance
ECE	ECN-related signaling
CWR	Congestion Window Reduced
SYN
tcp.flags.syn == 1

Used to identify TCP connection initiation.

FIN
tcp.flags.fin == 1

Used during normal TCP connection termination.

RST
tcp.flags.reset == 1

Used when a TCP connection is reset.

14. TCP Connection Termination

A TCP connection can normally be closed using FIN and ACK messages.

A simplified exchange is:

Client → Server : FIN
Server → Client : ACK
Server → Client : FIN
Client → Server : ACK

This is often called a TCP four-way termination.

An RST is different.

tcp.flags.reset == 1

A reset indicates an abrupt termination/reset.

15. TCP Troubleshooting

Wireshark provides useful TCP analysis indicators.

Retransmissions
tcp.analysis.retransmission

Retransmission means a TCP segment has been sent again.

Possible causes include:

Packet loss
Congestion
Wireless interference
Network problems
Receiver delays

A retransmission is not automatically proof of a network attack.

Duplicate ACK
tcp.analysis.duplicate_ack

Duplicate acknowledgments may indicate:

Packet loss
Out-of-order delivery
Network congestion
Out-of-Order Packets
tcp.analysis.out_of_order

Shows packets that Wireshark considers to have arrived out of sequence.

Zero Window
tcp.analysis.zero_window

Indicates that a receiver has advertised a TCP receive window of zero.

This can mean the receiving application or host is temporarily unable to accept more data.

16. UDP

UDP stands for:

User Datagram Protocol

UDP is a connectionless transport protocol.

Unlike TCP, UDP does not provide:

Three-way handshake
Guaranteed delivery
Ordering
TCP-style retransmission
TCP-style flow control

UDP has a very small header.

UDP Header
Field	Description
Source Port	Sender's port
Destination Port	Receiver's port
Length	UDP header + data length
Checksum	Error detection
Data	Application payload

Useful filter:

udp

Example:

udp.port == 53
17. DNS

DNS stands for:

Domain Name System

DNS converts domain names into IP addresses and can provide other types of information.

Example:

www.example.com
        ↓
93.184.216.34

DNS commonly uses:

UDP port 53

TCP can also be used for DNS in situations where TCP is appropriate.

Modern encrypted DNS may use protocols such as DNS over HTTPS or DNS over TLS.

DNS Query

A client asks:

What is the IP address of example.com?
DNS Response

The DNS server responds with the requested record.

Useful Filters
dns

All DNS traffic.

dns.flags.response == 0

DNS queries.

dns.flags.response == 1

DNS responses.

dns.qry.type == 1

A record queries.

dns.qry.type == 28

AAAA record queries.

dns.qry.name == "example.com"

Queries for a specific domain.

18. DHCP

DHCP stands for:

Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol

DHCP automatically provides network configuration to hosts.

It can provide:

IP address
Subnet mask
Default gateway
DNS server
Lease information

The common DHCP process is:

DORA

Meaning:

Discover
   ↓
Offer
   ↓
Request
   ↓
Acknowledge
1. DHCP Discover

Client searches for a DHCP server.

2. DHCP Offer

Server offers an IP configuration.

3. DHCP Request

Client requests the offered configuration.

4. DHCP ACK

Server confirms the configuration.

Useful Wireshark filter:

bootp

Wireshark commonly displays DHCP under the BOOTP protocol family.

