20. HTTPS and TLS

HTTPS is essentially HTTP protected by TLS.

HTTPS commonly uses:

TCP port 443

TLS provides encryption and helps protect application data in transit.

A simplified connection looks like:

TCP Handshake
      ↓
TLS Handshake
      ↓
Encrypted Application Data

Useful filter:

tls

TLS Client Hello:

tls.handshake.type == 1

The Client Hello is an important early message in the TLS handshake.

It can contain information such as:

Supported TLS versions
Cipher suites
Extensions
Server Name Indication (when present)
Supported groups
Signature algorithms

The exact fields depend on the TLS version and configuration.

Important Point

With normal HTTPS traffic, Wireshark can usually show metadata and TLS handshake information, but the application data is encrypted.

Therefore:

Seeing HTTPS packets in Wireshark does not mean that the webpage contents are automatically readable.
